# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored — never commit real keys.

# --- Supabase (optional) --------------------------------------------------
# Settings → API in your Supabase project. The ANON key is safe in the app
# bundle; every table is row-level-secured and scoped to the signed-in user.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=
EXPO_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# --- Catalogue push (local scripts only) ----------------------------------
# The SERVICE ROLE key bypasses every row-level policy. It belongs only in your
# shell when running scripts/push-catalogue.mjs — never in EXPO_PUBLIC_*, never
# in the app, never in a commit.
# SUPABASE_URL=
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Extraction (optional) ------------------------------------------------
# This key is the only thing that lets the app read a document at all. Without
# it there is no reader on any platform, web included, and the upload flow says
# so and asks for the details by hand — see src/features/extraction/ocr-provider.ts.
#
# With a key, a document is read by sending the photograph to Google's Gemini
# API. That is a real disclosure, not a detail: setting this key changes what the
# privacy screen tells the user, automatically, because the screen is generated
# from the provider that runs rather than from copy someone remembers to update.
#
# Note the prefix. EXPO_PUBLIC_ variables are inlined into the JS bundle at build
# time, so this key is readable by anyone who has the app. The Edge Function in
# supabase/functions/extract-w2 is the arrangement that actually keeps a key
# secret; this one is here because the main upload path has not moved to it yet.
EXPO_PUBLIC_GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Overrides the model. Only needed when the defaults stop working: Google
# retires model names ("no longer available to new users") and returns 503 on
# busy ones, so src/features/extraction/gemini-vision.ts tries a short list.
# EXPO_PUBLIC_GEMINI_MODEL=
